        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:26:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62145,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours can occur at any age (mean: 30&nbsp;years). Patients may present with signs or symptoms related to a pelvic mass or with estrogenic or (less commonly) androgenic manifestations, or Sertoli cell tumour may be an incidental finding. Rarely, renin, progesterone, or aldosterone production by the tumour may be responsible of symptoms {7427869}. A subset of patients have Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:26:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62146,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Sertoli cell tumours are rare {15644771}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:26:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62147,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:26:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62148,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>A subset of Sertoli cell tumours harbour <em>DICER1</em> mutations {26428316}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:26:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62149,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are unilateral neoplasms with a mean size of 8&nbsp;cm. They are typically solid with a tan to yellow cut surface, but they may be solid and cystic or rarely cystic. Haemorrhage and necrosis may be seen {7427869; 15644771}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:26:34"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62150,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 79,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "<p>Sertoli cell tumours may show a broad array of patterns, with tubules (either hollow or solid) present at least focally in most neoplasms. Other patterns include trabecular, diffuse, alveolar, pseudopapillary, retiform, and (rarely) pseudoendometrioid and spindled. Cuboidal or columnar cells usually have pale, sometimes lipid-rich to eosinophilic cytoplasm and bland oval to round nuclei, typically with a small nucleolus. Lipid-rich and oxyphilic tumours may be associated with Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome. Rarely, bizarre nuclei or marked cytological atypia and brisk mitotic activity are seen. The stroma ranges from scant to abundant and hyalinized. A minor granulosa component or areas resembling a sex cord tumour with annular tubules may be seen, the latter in patients with Peutz&ndash;Jeghers syndrome {26621753}. The presence of occasional Leydig cells within the tumour does not exclude the diagnosis of Sertoli cell tumour. Most tumours are positive for inhibin, SF1, calretinin, CD99, WT1, and broad-spectrum cytokeratins. EMA, CK7, PAX8, GATA3, and chromogranin are negative {17255771; 17721194; 18753972; 19033865}. Features predictive of malignant behaviour include size &gt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;cm, mitotic count of &gt;&nbsp;2&nbsp;mitoses/mm<sup>2</sup> (equating to &gt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;mitoses/10&nbsp;HPF of 0.55&nbsp;mm in diameter and 0.24&nbsp;mm<sup>2</sup> in area), nuclear atypia, and necrosis {15644771}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-07-23T15:52:40"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
